And if you're on a podcast
service, please leave a comment.

00:00:21.360 --> 00:00:22.620
I really do appreciate it.

00:00:23.070 --> 00:00:24.960
So when I wrote I should...

00:00:25.020 --> 00:00:29.070
yeah, when I wrote my book, here
is of course the English version.

00:00:29.340 --> 00:00:29.729
Here's...

00:00:29.859 --> 00:00:32.040
I just happen to have the
Chinese version nearby.

00:00:33.000 --> 00:00:34.330
A Personal Guide to Language Learning.

00:00:34.350 --> 00:00:35.489
The linguist, I called it.

00:00:36.690 --> 00:00:40.800
Linguist means someone who, you
know, speaks many languages.

00:00:40.890 --> 00:00:42.690
That's the original meaning
of the word linguist.

00:00:42.690 --> 00:00:45.810
Whenever I say that, I get attacked
by people who study linguistics.

00:00:46.050 --> 00:00:49.410
But if you open up a dictionary
that in fact is the number one

00:00:49.410 --> 00:00:51.330
meaning of the word linguist.

00:00:51.675 --> 00:00:54.635
Most English speakers are not
familiar with the word polyglot.

00:00:54.855 --> 00:00:59.335
Anyway, when I wrote that book, I
said the most important, sort of,

00:01:00.045 --> 00:01:04.709
measure of how well we know a language
is the number of words we know.

00:01:04.980 --> 00:01:06.690
Vocabulary is key.

00:01:06.990 --> 00:01:11.070
Words and phrases, but phrases consist
of words, so you have to have words.

00:01:11.580 --> 00:01:16.140
And to be fluent, you actually
need a lot of words because the

00:01:16.145 --> 00:01:17.820
native speaker has a lot of words.

00:01:18.000 --> 00:01:23.280
So if you're exposing yourself to a
native speaker, speakers in conversation

00:01:23.490 --> 00:01:28.020
or watching a movie or listening to a
podcast, all of which are tremendous ways

00:01:28.020 --> 00:01:32.940
of improving in the language, you need
a lot of words, and while I sometimes

00:01:32.940 --> 00:01:36.750
hear people say, Well, I can be fluent
with 500 words, because 500 words

00:01:36.755 --> 00:01:41.670
accounts for 60% of most content, but
in fact that's not true because the low

00:01:41.670 --> 00:01:46.560
frequency words and frequency declines
very quickly, as I've said before.

00:01:47.085 --> 00:01:52.155
They're key to, you know, the
gist of what any content is about.

00:01:52.965 --> 00:01:55.245
The high frequency
words are not a problem.

00:01:55.250 --> 00:02:00.285
They repeat often and very quickly get to
know them, but you need these other words.

00:02:00.290 --> 00:02:03.525
You need a high sort of vocabulary level.

00:02:03.585 --> 00:02:05.505
So, how do we achieve that?

00:02:05.565 --> 00:02:09.824
I think one of the things that
can help us is if we accept the

00:02:09.824 --> 00:02:11.295
fact that we're gonna forget.

00:02:11.925 --> 00:02:16.935
Not only accept the fact that we're gonna
forget, but realize that forgetting is

00:02:16.935 --> 00:02:19.515
a key part of the strategy of learning.

00:02:19.695 --> 00:02:23.204
And, and there are a number of references
and I did a bit of Googling, so.

00:02:23.515 --> 00:02:27.235
You know, I, I, I Googled
Proust, Marcel Proust, the, the

00:02:27.235 --> 00:02:29.035
French, uh, you know, novelist.

00:02:29.545 --> 00:02:34.105
And he has this term ... the
creative effect of forgetting.

00:02:34.285 --> 00:02:37.495
And sometimes those things that
we don't deliberately try to

00:02:37.500 --> 00:02:39.415
remember, they kind of stay there.

00:02:39.475 --> 00:02:41.245
That was his intuition.

00:02:42.075 --> 00:02:47.234
Now if you wanna see a more scientific,
sort of, explanation of that concept,

00:02:47.505 --> 00:02:54.554
I uh, uh, recommend that you Google
for Robert Bjork uh, B J O R K and

00:02:54.560 --> 00:02:58.785
the word forgetting, and you'll see a
number of of videos that he has done,

00:02:58.875 --> 00:03:04.829
which explains why forgetting is such
an important part of learning that

00:03:05.370 --> 00:03:09.269
in our memory we have the ability to
retrieve something, but we also have

00:03:09.269 --> 00:03:11.609
this issue of what we have in storage.

00:03:11.670 --> 00:03:12.180
Okay.

00:03:12.540 --> 00:03:18.390
And to some extent, if we try to train
the ability to retrieve a limited set

00:03:18.510 --> 00:03:24.285
of information, words, for example, we
aren't building up that storage, larger

00:03:24.285 --> 00:03:29.535
storage of material that we may not be
able to retrieve right now, but it's there

00:03:29.535 --> 00:03:32.265
potentially available for us to retrieve.

00:03:32.925 --> 00:03:37.155
And he explains very clearly that once
we forget something and we retrieve

00:03:37.155 --> 00:03:41.235
it again and maybe we re-forget it
again and we retrieve it again, we're

00:03:41.235 --> 00:03:46.915
expanding that sort of reserve of
things that we can uh, uh, retrieve.

00:03:46.915 --> 00:03:51.855
We are developing a large vocabulary
and to do well in languages,

00:03:51.855 --> 00:03:53.415
we need a large vocabulary.

00:03:53.415 --> 00:03:59.325
Even if our large passive vocabulary
is not available to us to use or

00:03:59.325 --> 00:04:03.894
gradually becomes available for us to
use it is enabling us to understand,

00:04:04.454 --> 00:04:08.954
to understand, you know, interesting
conversations or interesting sources

00:04:08.954 --> 00:04:11.355
of, of information in the language.

00:04:11.684 --> 00:04:16.769
So I think sometimes in schools we
are trained to not wanna forget.

00:04:17.010 --> 00:04:19.530
We're trained, you know, the
teacher teaches something and

00:04:19.530 --> 00:04:21.090
we've now gotta try to remember it.

00:04:21.149 --> 00:04:25.320
Or we have tests on what was said
in the story and we're very much

00:04:25.320 --> 00:04:29.190
oriented towards trying to get
it right, trying to retrieve it.

00:04:29.430 --> 00:04:34.234
And I sometimes think we would be better
advised to accept that we're gonna forget.

00:04:34.724 --> 00:04:39.585
And accept that the process of forgetting
is, is building up this reserve

00:04:39.825 --> 00:04:44.474
that our ability to retrieve that
information is, is going to improve.

00:04:44.625 --> 00:04:48.585
But in the meantime, we also
have to build up that reserve of

00:04:48.734 --> 00:04:53.835
words that we will eventually be
able to access better and better.

00:04:53.865 --> 00:04:58.205
Robert Bjork also, uh, has
videos on interleaving.

00:04:58.500 --> 00:05:03.990
Pointing out again how important it
is to to learn things in different

00:05:03.990 --> 00:05:07.230
environments and at different
times and in different ways.

00:05:07.470 --> 00:05:10.170
Read about the same subject
matter in different books.

00:05:10.200 --> 00:05:12.630
I've called this grazing in the past.

00:05:12.990 --> 00:05:16.500
Not worrying too much about
what we retain, but making sure

00:05:16.500 --> 00:05:20.040
that we expose ourselves to this
information in different ways.

00:05:20.070 --> 00:05:24.795
He says, for example, people are
sometimes advised to always learn

00:05:24.795 --> 00:05:26.445
something in the same place.

00:05:26.445 --> 00:05:30.615
So if you study, study in your library
or study in your, I don't know, kitchen

00:05:30.615 --> 00:05:35.325
or whatever, and if you study there and
there is research to show that if people,

00:05:35.385 --> 00:05:38.445
you know, having studied something, if
they go back to the place where they

00:05:38.450 --> 00:05:40.545
studied it, they'll remember it better.

00:05:40.865 --> 00:05:47.160
However, you're better off to not
do that, to study it here and then

00:05:47.160 --> 00:05:48.450
somewhere else and somewhere else.

00:05:48.660 --> 00:05:52.380
So your ability, ability to retrieve
the information if you go to a

00:05:52.380 --> 00:05:54.900
different setting may be less.

00:05:54.930 --> 00:06:01.680
However, by studying that same material
in different environments, even you are

00:06:01.680 --> 00:06:04.760
building up your reserve of information.

00:06:05.250 --> 00:06:08.430
And that will eventually
give you more information.

00:06:08.520 --> 00:06:13.770
Even though in the short run your ability
to retrieve the information is reduced.

00:06:13.890 --> 00:06:19.200
A good example of learning things in
different environments, learning and

00:06:19.200 --> 00:06:26.280
forgetting, building up our reserve
is the way I use LingQ on my iPad.

00:06:26.910 --> 00:06:30.090
Could be on your iPhone or
Android as well, or on the web.

00:06:30.690 --> 00:06:36.480
So if I look at our mini stories, for
example, lesson 59, I can read it in

00:06:36.480 --> 00:06:42.300
the full lesson mode or I can go, you
know, uh, so here, for example, here's

00:06:42.305 --> 00:06:46.650
page, the next page, but I can also
look at it one sentence at a time.

00:06:46.980 --> 00:06:50.670
So that's a different experience,
varying the experience.

00:06:50.670 --> 00:06:53.310
The way we do things is
a good thing to do here.

00:06:53.310 --> 00:06:58.410
For example, I have a number of
words that I am trying to learn.

00:06:59.070 --> 00:07:02.510
I, I don't spend a lot of time
deliberately trying to remember

00:07:02.515 --> 00:07:07.110
these words, but I can still go
through some of the, you know,

00:07:07.380 --> 00:07:10.440
exercises here right after the page.

00:07:10.710 --> 00:07:21.090
So I might ... is accessible I hope.

00:07:21.610 --> 00:07:35.234
...
okay ... and I don't try too
hard to remember these things.

00:07:35.234 --> 00:07:37.734
I just kind of go through them.

00:07:37.734 --> 00:07:41.234
... in other words, being no,
being as seeing how...

00:07:42.570 --> 00:07:43.409
so I get it wrong.

00:07:43.530 --> 00:07:45.150
It doesn't matter if I get it wrong.

00:07:45.150 --> 00:07:49.830
Continue, and then in what you will
soon be seeing is the opportunity then

00:07:49.830 --> 00:07:52.560
to, to reassemble this into a sentence.

00:07:52.650 --> 00:07:55.830
I do it not for all the text,
but I do it to some extent.

00:07:56.070 --> 00:08:00.599
But the key thing is I don't worry
about what I don't understand.

00:08:00.719 --> 00:08:02.940
I don't worry about what I forget.

00:08:03.150 --> 00:08:04.710
I vary how I do things.

00:08:04.710 --> 00:08:07.049
Sometimes I do it in full text mode.

00:08:07.049 --> 00:08:09.660
Sometimes I do, you know,
a sentence at a time.

00:08:09.840 --> 00:08:12.810
Sometimes I do easy content,
such as the mini stories.

00:08:12.990 --> 00:08:14.850
Sometimes I do more difficult content.

00:08:15.090 --> 00:08:18.270
I never worry about what I
forget because I know that

00:08:18.270 --> 00:08:21.180
forgetting is the key to learning.

00:08:22.080 --> 00:08:25.560
So I just wanted to mention that
because a lot of people seem to get

00:08:25.560 --> 00:08:27.140
frustrated when they forget things.

00:08:27.870 --> 00:08:29.340
Forgetting is not a bad thing.

00:08:29.650 --> 00:08:33.615
Forgetting ... forgetting is a good thing.

00:08:33.705 --> 00:08:38.054
You wanna forget and relearn
and hear it in another context

00:08:38.054 --> 00:08:39.765
and read it in another context.

00:08:39.975 --> 00:08:44.415
Trust the fact that eventually the
vocabulary will stick, but expose yourself

00:08:44.420 --> 00:08:50.170
to a variety of contexts and don't just
try to learn one, sort of, limited group

00:08:50.200 --> 00:08:56.560
of vocabulary items in one specific
way, but rather build up your vocabulary

00:08:56.620 --> 00:08:58.510
reserve becasue you're gonna need it.

00:08:58.690 --> 00:09:01.630
So increase your vocabulary by forgetting.

00:09:02.470 --> 00:09:03.520
Thank you for listening.

00:09:03.850 --> 00:09:04.480
Bye for now.

